{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DYEkxKpaXCSXDgy6RfenE1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t/* SPDX-License-Identifier: SunMicrosystems */\n2\t/* Copyright (C) 1993 by Sun Microsystems, Inc. All rights reserved. */\n3\t\n4\t/**\n5\t *\n6\t * This file contains a set of functions used by multiple procedures as\n7\t * internal functions. These procedures should not be accessed directly by a\n8\t * user. Note that all procedures are either macros or static inline procedures.\n9\t *\n10\t * Synopsis\n11\t * ========\n12\t *\n13\t * .. code-block:: c\n14\t *\n15\t *     #include \"tools.h\"\n16\t *     EXTRACT_WORDS(ix0,ix1,d);                // this macro has no return value, the inputs are expected to be (int, int, double)\n17\t *     GET_HIGH_WORD(i,d);                      // this macro has no return value, the inputs are expected to be (int, double)\n18\t *     GET_LOW_WORD(i,d);                       // this macro has no return value, the inputs are expected to be (int, double)\n19\t *     INSERT_WORDS(d,ix0,ix1);                 // this macro has no return value, the inputs are expected to be (double, int, int)\n20\t *     SET_HIGH_WORD(d,v);                      // this macro has no return value, the inputs are expected to be (double, int)\n21\t *     SET_LOW_WORD(d,v);                       // this macro has no return value, the inputs are expected to be (double, int)\n22\t *     GET_FLOAT_WORD(i,f);                     // this macro has no return value, the inputs are expected to be (int, float)\n23\t *     SET_FLOAT_WORD(f,i);                     // this macro has no return value, the inputs are expected to be (float, int)\n24\t *     SAFE_RIGHT_SHIFT(op,amt);                // this macros return value has the same type as input `op`, the inputs are expected to be integer types\n25\t *     double __forced_calculation(double x);\n26\t *     float __forced_calculationf(float x);\n27\t *     double __raise_invalid(void);\n28\t *     float __raise_invalidf(void);\n29\t *     double __raise_div_by_zero(double x);\n30\t *     float __raise_div_by_zerof(float x);\n31\t *     double __raise_overflow(double x);\n32\t *     float __raise_overflowf(float x);\n33\t *     double __raise_underflow(double x);\n34\t *     float __raise_underflowf(float x);\n35\t *     double __raise_inexact(double x);\n36\t *     float __raise_inexactf(float x);\n37\t *     int __issignaling(double x);\n38\t *     int __issignalingf(float x);\n39\t *     REAL_PART(z);                            // this macros return value has the non-complex type of input `z`, the input is expected to be a complex floating-point datum\n40\t *     IMAG_PART(z);                            // this macros return value has the non-complex type of input `z`, the input is expected to be a complex floating-point datum\n41\t *\n42\t * Description\n43\t * ===========\n44\t *\n45\t * ``EXTRACT_WORDS`` is a macro to extract two integer words from a double\n46\t * floating-point datum.\n47\t *\n48\t * ``GET_HIGH_WORD`` is a macro to extract only the highword of the two integer\n49\t * words from a double floating-point datum.\n50\t *\n51\t * ``GET_LOW_WORD`` is a macro to extract only the lowword of the two integer\n52\t * words from a double floating-point datum.\n53\t *\n54\t * ``INSERT_WORDS`` is a macro to insert two integer words into a double\n55\t * floating-point datum.\n56\t *\n57\t * ``SET_HIGH_WORD`` is a macro to insert only the highword of the two integer\n58\t * words into a double floating-point datum.\n59\t *\n60\t * ``SET_LOW_WORD`` is a macro to insert only the lowword of the two integer\n61\t * words into a double floating-point datum.\n62\t *\n63\t * ``GET_FLOAT_WORD`` is a macro to extract the integer representation from a\n64\t * single floating-point datum.\n65\t *\n66\t * ``SET_FLOAT_WORD`` is a macro to insert the integer representation into a\n67\t * single floating-point datum.\n68\t *\n69\t * ``SAFE_RIGHT_SHIFT`` is a macro to avoid undefined behaviour that can arise\n70\t * if the amount of a right shift is exactly equal to the size of the shifted\n71\t * operand. If the amount is equal to the size the macro returns 0.\n72\t *\n73\t * ``__forced_calculation`` is a function to force the execution of the input\n74\t * to go through the :ref:`FPU <ABBR>`. The input for this function is usually\n75\t * an arithmetic operation and not a single value. At the moment the function\n76\t * is only used by others within this file and not expected to be called from\n77\t * outside.\n78\t *\n79\t * ``__raise_invalid`` is a function to force the :ref:`FPU <ABBR>` to generate\n80\t * an ``invalid operation`` exception.\n81\t *\n82\t * ``__raise_div_by_zero`` is a function to force the :ref:`FPU <ABBR>` to\n83\t * generate an ``divide by zero`` exception.\n84\t *\n85\t * ``__raise_overflow`` is a function to force the :ref:`FPU <ABBR>` to\n86\t * generate an ``overflow`` exception.\n87\t *\n88\t * ``__raise_underflow`` is a function to force the :ref:`FPU <ABBR>` to\n89\t * generate an ``underflow`` exception. Even though the library usually does\n90\t * not care about this exception for qualification purposes, the library still\n91\t * tries to conform to the standards (mainly POSIX) in regards to where the\n92\t * exception shall be raised intentionally. In those places this function is\n93\t * used.\n94\t *\n95\t * ``__raise_inexact`` is a function to force the :ref:`FPU <ABBR>` to generate\n96\t * an ``inexact`` exception. Even though the library usually does not care\n97\t * about this exception for qualification purposes, the library still tries to\n98\t * conform to the standards (mainly POSIX) in regards to where the exception\n99\t * shall be raised intentionally. In those places this function is used.\n100\t *\n101\t * ``__issignaling`` is a function to check if a value is a signaling NaN.\n102\t *\n103\t * ``REAL_PART`` is a macro to extract only the real part of a complex\n104\t * floating-point datum.\n105\t *\n106\t * ``IMAG_PART`` is a macro to extract only the imaginary part of a complex\n107\t * floating-point datum.\n108\t *\n109\t * Mathematical Function\n110\t * =====================\n111\t *\n112\t * .. math::\n113\t *\n114\t *    EXTRACT\\_WORDS_{ix0}(d) &= \\text{highword of } d  \\\\\n115\t *    EXTRACT\\_WORDS_{ix1}(d) &= \\text{lowword of } d  \\\\\n116\t *    GET\\_HIGH\\_WORD_{i}(d) &= \\text{highword of } d  \\\\\n117\t *    GET\\_LOW\\_WORD_{i}(d) &= \\text{lowword of } d  \\\\\n118\t *    SET\\_HIGH\\_WORD_{\\text{highword of } d}(d, v) &= d \\text{ with highword } v  \\\\\n119\t *    SET\\_LOW\\_WORD_{\\text{lowword of } d}(d, v) &= d \\text{ with lowword } v  \\\\\n120\t *    GET\\_FLOAT\\_WORD_{i}(d) &= \\text{integer representation of } d  \\\\\n121\t *    SET\\_FLOAT\\_WORD_{d}(i) &= \\text{floating-point representation of } i  \\\\\n122\t *    SAFE\\_RIGHT\\_SHIFT(op, amt) &= \\left\\{\\begin{array}{ll} op \\gg amt, & amt < \\text{size of } op \\\\ 0, & otherwise \\end{array}\\right.  \\\\\n123\t *    \\_\\_forced\\_calculation(x) &= x  \\\\\n124\t *    \\_\\_raise\\_invalid() &= NaN  \\\\\n125\t *    \\_\\_raise\\_div\\_by\\_zero(x) &= \\left\\{\\begin{array}{ll} -Inf, & x \\in \\mathbb{F}^{-} \\\\ +Inf, & otherwise \\end{array}\\right.  \\\\\n126\t *    \\_\\_raise\\_overflow(x) &= \\left\\{\\begin{array}{ll} -Inf, & x \\in \\mathbb{F}^{-} \\\\ +Inf, & otherwise \\end{array}\\right.  \\\\\n127\t *    \\_\\_raise\\_underflow(x) &= \\left\\{\\begin{array}{ll} -0.0, & x \\in \\mathbb{F}^{-} \\\\ +0.0, & otherwise \\end{array}\\right.  \\\\\n128\t *    \\_\\_raise\\_inexact(x) &= x  \\\\\n129\t *    \\_\\_issignaling(x) &= \\left\\{\\begin{array}{ll} 1, & x = sNaN \\\\ 0, & otherwise \\end{array}\\right.  \\\\\n130\t *    REAL\\_PART(z) &= \\Re(z)  \\\\\n131\t *    IMAG\\_PART(z) &= \\Im(z)\n132\t *\n133\t * Returns\n134\t * =======\n135\t *\n136\t * ``EXTRACT_WORDS`` has no return value. It places the two extracted integer\n137\t * words into the parameters ``ix0`` and ``ix1``.\n138\t *\n139\t * ``GET_HIGH_WORD`` has no return value. It places the extracted integer word\n140\t * into the parameter ``i``.\n141\t *\n142\t * ``GET_LOW_WORD`` has no return value. It places the extracted integer word\n143\t * into the parameter ``i``.\n144\t *\n145\t * ``INSERT_WORDS`` has no return value. It places the created double\n146\t * floating-point datum into the parameter ``d``.\n147\t *\n148\t * ``SET_HIGH_WORD`` has no return value. It places the created double\n149\t * floating-point datum into the parameter ``d``.\n150\t *\n151\t * ``SET_LOW_WORD`` has no return value. It places the created double\n152\t * floating-point datum into the parameter ``d``.\n153\t *\n154\t * ``GET_FLOAT_WORD`` has no return value. It places the extracted integer word\n155\t * into the parameter ``i``.\n156\t *\n157\t * ``SET_FLOAT_WORD`` has no return value. It places the created single\n158\t * floating-point datum into the parameter ``f``.\n159\t *\n160\t * ``SAFE_RIGHT_SHIFT`` returns the value ``op`` right shifted by ``amt`` if\n161\t * ``amt`` is smaller than the size of ``op``, otherwise it returns zero.\n162\t *\n163\t * ``__forced_calculation`` returns the input value, or rather the result of\n164\t * the arithmetic calculation used as an input.\n165\t *\n166\t * ``__raise_invalid`` returns ``NaN``.\n167\t *\n168\t * ``__raise_div_by_zero`` returns ``-Inf`` for negative inputs and ``+Inf``\n169\t * for positive inputs.\n170\t *\n171\t * ``__raise_overflow`` returns ``-Inf`` for negative inputs and ``+Inf`` for\n172\t * positive inputs.\n173\t *\n174\t * ``__raise_underflow`` returns ``-0.0`` for negative inputs and ``+0.0`` for\n175\t * positive inputs.\n176\t *\n177\t * ``__raise_inexact`` returns the input value.\n178\t *\n179\t * ``__issignaling`` returns ``1`` if the input value is a signaling ``NaN``,\n180\t * else ``0``.\n181\t *\n182\t * ``REAL_PART`` returns the real part of the input as a floating-point datum\n183\t * of the adequate size.\n184\t *\n185\t * ``IMAG_PART`` returns the imaginary part of the input as a floating-point\n186\t * datum of the adequate size.\n187\t *\n188\t * Exceptions\n189\t * ==========\n190\t *\n191\t * The macros do not raise exceptions.\n192\t *\n193\t * The functions raise the exceptions they are named after, it is their sole\n194\t * purpose. Additionally the procedures ``__raise_overflow`` and\n195\t * ``__raise_underflow`` may raise ``inexact``.\n196\t *\n197\t */\n198\t\n199\t#ifndef LIBMCS_TOOLS_H\n200\t#define LIBMCS_TOOLS_H\n201\t\n202\t#include <limits.h>\n203\t#include <stdbool.h>\n204\t#include <stdint.h>\n205\t\n206\t#include <math.h>\n207\t\n208\t#ifdef __LIBMCS_WANT_COMPLEX\n209\t    #include <complex.h>\n210\t#endif /* __LIBMCS_WANT_COMPLEX */\n211\t\n212\t/* A union which permits us to convert between a double and two 32 bit\n213\t   ints.  */\n214\t\n215\t#ifdef __IEEE_BIG_ENDIAN\n216\t\n217\ttypedef union {\n218\t    double value;\n219\t    struct {\n220\t        uint32_t msw;\n221\t        uint32_t lsw;\n222\t    } parts;\n223\t} ieee_double_shape_type;\n224\t\n225\t#endif\n226\t\n227\t#ifdef __IEEE_LITTLE_ENDIAN\n228\t\n229\ttypedef union {\n230\t    double value;\n231\t    struct {\n232\t        uint32_t lsw;\n233\t        uint32_t msw;\n234\t    } parts;\n235\t} ieee_double_shape_type;\n236\t\n237\t#endif\n238\t\n239\t/* Get two 32 bit ints from a double.  */\n240\t\n241\t#define EXTRACT_WORDS(ix0,ix1,d)                        \\\n242\t    do {                                                \\\n243\t        ieee_double_shape_type ew_u;                    \\\n244\t        ew_u.value = (d);                               \\\n245\t        (ix0) = ew_u.parts.msw;                         \\\n246\t        (ix1) = ew_u.parts.lsw;                         \\\n247\t    } while (0 == 1)\n248\t\n249\t/* Get the more significant 32 bit int from a double.  */\n250\t\n251\t#define GET_HIGH_WORD(i,d)             